# Source documents downloaded by scripts/fetch_sources.py: ~250 MB of personnel
# roster PDFs and budgeted-employee spreadsheets straight from nebraska.edu.
# Reproducible from data/source_manifest.json, which IS committed and carries a
# SHA-256 per file, so a re-fetch can be proven identical to the one that built
# the CSVs. Same split as ne-campaign-finance: raw stays out, derived comes in.
data/raw/

# Intermediates. roster_* and appointments_* are each one source read on its
# own; salaries_* is the reconciled result of the two and is what gets
# committed. Regenerate with parse_roster_pdf.py / parse_xlsx.py -- about 30
# minutes for all 17 years.
data/by_year/roster_*.csv
data/by_year/appointments_*.csv

# The row-by-row reconciliation trail: 158,280 comparisons, 10 MB, and almost
# all of it is rows recording that the two sources agreed. The interesting part
# -- what disagreed and why -- is summarised in reconciliation_summary.csv,
# which IS committed. Regenerate the detail with reconcile.py.
data/reconciliation/recon_*.csv

# 203,400 rows, 28 MB, and nothing in it that the committed files do not
# already hold: it is the per-year salaries_*.csv stacked with a person id
# attached. The page reads data/site/history/ instead, and people.csv carries
# the one-row-per-person summary. Regenerate with build_people.py in seconds.
data/salary_history.csv
